{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And My covenant shall be in your flesh for an everlasting covenant.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the reason for circumcision is that it is a fixed sign in the flesh upon the faith in the blessed Name and His unity, which will not change nor depart from him, whether in life or after death; and behold, it enters into the faith of unity, in the manner of what is written: 'to be to you for an Elohim and for your seed after you.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: the commandment of circumcision is like the matter of an offering, and just as the blood of the offering effects atonement upon the altar, so the blood of circumcision effects atonement; therefore its commandment is on the eighth day, for the offering is not valid until the eighth day, as it is said (Leviticus 22:27) 'and from the eighth day and onward it shall be accepted,' and just as it is written of an offering (Exodus 29:33) 'and they shall eat them with which atonement was made,' that the eating of the offering is for atonement, so Israel make a feast on the day of circumcision. And its power is still greater than that of the offering, for the offering is from one's property while circumcision is from one's body, in a limb in which all the tendons of the limbs are bound, which for this reason is called 'the head of the body,' that is, the head of the entire body, and therefore it is accounted to him as a binding and a sacrifice, as though he bound himself, as it is written (Psalms 50:5) 'I have cut My covenant with Me upon a sacrifice.'" "<b>And in Bereshit</b> Rabbah (Genesis 3:24), 'and the flame of the sword that turned' — Rav Huna and the Rabbis: Rav Huna said in the name of Rabbi Abba, 'the sword — this is circumcision,' as it is said (Joshua 5:2) 'make yourself flint swords.' The Rabbis say, 'the sword — this is Torah,' as it is said (Psalms 149:6) 'and a two-edged sword in their hand.' Rabbi Abdimi said: 'and the flame of the sword' — the flame, this is Gehenna, which brands a person from his head to his feet; 'the sword,' this is circumcision. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: 'Who saves My children from the blazing fire? You must say: this is circumcision.' (Proverbs 31:21) 'She does not fear for her household from snow, for all her household is clothed in scarlet' — 'snow,' this is Gehenna, as it is said (Psalms 68:15–16) 'it snows in Zalmon'; in the merit of circumcision — 'for all her household is clothed in scarlet,' this is the blood of circumcision." "<b>And in Pirkei</b> Rabbi Eliezer, chapter 29: Abraham circumcised himself on Yom HaKippurim, as it is written regarding Yom HaKippurim (Leviticus 23:28) 'and no manner of work shall you do on this very same day,' and so it says (Genesis 17:26) 'on this very same day Abraham was circumcised' — you must say: on Yom HaKippurim our father Abraham was circumcised. And every single year the Holy One, blessed be He, sees the blood of the covenant of our father Abraham and atones for all the iniquities of Israel, as it is said (Leviticus 16:30) 'for on this day He shall atone for you, to cleanse you from all your sins, before HaShem you shall be cleansed.'" "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): it is well known that cutting a limb weakens the power of that limb, and there is no limb among all the limbs of the body whose cutting from the beginning of its formation does not result, by nature, in a diminution of its power. Therefore the commandment of circumcision came, for it is a cause to weaken the power of desire and diminishes the pleasure of intercourse, as the Sages, blessed be their memory, held: 'one who lies with an uncircumcised man finds it hard to separate,' and on account of circumcision the perpetuation of seed is not thereby nullified, for the natural matter remains according to its nature, while it is safeguarded from what is superfluous. And the reason the commandment comes at eight days is that beforehand he is very weak and in the utmost moistness, as though he were still in the womb, and if he is left until he grows, the matter would come to nullification — thus explained the Rambam, of blessed memory." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah, <b>and My covenant shall be in your flesh for an everlasting covenant.</b> He says: <b>My covenant in your flesh</b> shall be a likeness <b>for an everlasting covenant</b> — that is, what is called צדיק יסוד עולם ('the Righteous One, the Foundation of the world'), and concerning him it is said 'an everlasting covenant of salt,' for He is the sustenance of the world. For Scripture should properly have said 'and My covenant shall be in your flesh לעולם' (forever), but it said <b>לברית עולם</b> ('for an everlasting covenant'), and this 'everlasting covenant' is between the Legs called נצח and הוד, and concerning this came the saying that illuminates the eyes of the heart, which the Sages of Truth, may their memory be for a blessing, said (Lamentations 2:1): ״ולא זכר הדום רגליו ביום אפו״ — the footstool between His Legs. And because the Malkhut is adjacent to them, opposite which is the periah (uncovering), therefore they said: 'one who circumcises but does not perform periah — it is as though he did not circumcise,' because he who does not do so separates and cuts off, and behold the perfection of the mitzvah is in both of them — it is the unification (yihud), and this is clear."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {2 "Citation reads Genesis 3:24, referring to Bereshit Rabbah on Genesis 3:24 (the verse about the flaming sword); normalized.", 3 "Abbreviation יוה\"כ resolved as Yom HaKippurim."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:13:10.457237867Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}